<figure class="pull-right"><img height="2760" sizes="(max-width: 750px) calc(100vw - 60px), (max-width: 1010px) 45vw, 345px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/tv-watchlist-app-dark-mode-show-list-6c-scaled.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/tv-watchlist-app-dark-mode-show-list-6c-scaled.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/tv-watchlist-app-dark-mode-show-list-6c-scaled.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/tv-watchlist-app-dark-mode-show-list-6c-scaled.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/tv-watchlist-app-dark-mode-show-list-6c-scaled.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1977" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/tv-watchlist-app-dark-mode-show-list-6c-scaled.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Dark-mode TV watchlist app screenshot listing shows with next episodes, services and episodes left." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>We live in a world with so many monolithic apps and services, dominated by giant companies. So one of my favorite things is to explore little software niches that haven&#8217;t been captured by the major players. Instead of one or two pretty samey apps, you can end up with a whole forest of wild variations on the theme, each fueled by developers making specific choices about how to present information and serve users.</p>
<p>When I compare these apps, I realize that there&#8217;s really no right or wrong—there are just choices, some of which might work for me, some of which won&#8217;t. I&#8217;m not offended when an app isn&#8217;t made for me, but I can be delighted when an app does slide perfectly into my life.</p>
<p>After a year of using <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2016/02/keeping-on-top-of-peak-tv-with-television-time/">Television Time</a> (not to be confused with the now-defunct <a href="https://sixcolors.com/member/2019/01/applications-folder-tv-time/">TV Time</a>) to log my TV viewing and (more importantly) let me keep track of what we&#8217;re watching next and what we&#8217;d like to get to next, I discovered an app that just served me better.</p>
<p>That&#8217;s <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/skipper-tv-movie-tracker/id6780667490">Skipper</a>, Yoav Zoldan&#8217;s new app, available for $30/year or $3/month. What sold me on it is that its primary screen is a list of shows I&#8217;m watching with the next episode marked, sorted in a way that makes sense to me. I only want to interact with the app in a handful of ways: seeing what to watch next, checking the box indicating that we watched it, and occasionally searching to add a show that I might be interested in watching next.</p>
<p>Television Time required more swiping around and entering detail views, and one thing I&#8217;ve discovered about myself is that I prefer apps that are optimized to present all the information I want in one place, and which limit the number of swipes and taps required to navigate around. Skipper hit that sweet spot for me—your mileage, of course, may vary.</p>
<figure><img height="645" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/skipper-services-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/skipper-services-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/skipper-services-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/skipper-services-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/skipper-services-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1386" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/skipper-services-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Dark-themed streaming dashboard: &quot;3 Services you could pause this month: BritBox, YouTube Premium, and YouTube TV: nothing to watch this month.&quot; Stats: 31 active shows, 9 services needed, 0 on hold. A heatmap shows show demand per service, Nov through Apr." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>Skipper has a lot of other features, including a very clever one that compares your watch list to your streaming service subscriptions and can nudge you to drop that service you&#8217;re not actually using, complete with a calendar of when shows you watch will be returning. (I know, I know, I shouldn&#8217;t have subscribed to BritBox. I have no time to watch it!)</p>
<p>It&#8217;s not entirely accurate—it doesn&#8217;t seem to understand that I watch sports on YouTube TV and things that are not shows on YouTube Premium—but it&#8217;s a smart feature that could help a lot of people who are not inveterate streaming subscription sickos like me.</p>
<p>Just as there are many TV-tracking apps, there are many, many podcast apps. I&#8217;ve <a href="https://sixcolors.com/link/2024/07/overcast-turns-10/">been using Overcast for ages</a>, and it just fits into how I want to listen to podcasts. I spend as little time as possible picking what to listen to (there&#8217;s my optimization streak again) and have Overcast configured to give me that.</p>
<p>But there have been other podcast apps that serve other, equally valid, points of view. <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2016/08/castro-2-inbox-triage-for-podcast-episodes/">Castro</a> really made its name by not just offering a beautiful interface, but a clever &#8220;triage&#8221; system that brings new episodes into an inbox. Then the user manually decides what&#8217;s worth adding to the play queue. Clearly that approach clicked with a lot of people, making Castro a fan favorite even through its ongoing business challenges and <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2023/11/castro-podcast-app-goes-down-and-may-be-dying/">different owners</a>.</p>
<figure><img height="2005" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/06-queue-framed-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/06-queue-framed-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/06-queue-framed-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/06-queue-framed-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/06-queue-framed-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="2760" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/06-queue-framed-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="A podcast app on a folding phone in landscape. The left shows The Talk Show episode &quot;456: 'Lubricate the Inside of the Hinge', With Joanna Stern&quot; playing. The right shows a Queue with Up Next episodes from Analog(ue), Upgrade, Waveform, and The Vergecast." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>Which brings me to an impressive new podcast app that&#8217;s been designed to appeal especially to Castro fans: Ben Rice McCarthy&#8217;s <a href="https://benricemccarthy.ghost.io/tripod/">Tripod</a>. Ben is a really talented designer, and the app looks gorgeous and works well. It&#8217;s built around the same Inbox concept as Castro, so Castro users will feel right at home. I got to beta test Ben&#8217;s app for a while, and was incredibly impressed—especially at how it works on the iPhone Duo.</p>
<p>That all said, I am just not the target audience for Tripod—and that&#8217;s okay. The way Overcast works for podcasts is the way I want to work. Quite frankly, the idea of having another Inbox to check does not fill my heart with joy, even if the podcasts found therein would bring me that joy. That&#8217;s on me, not on Ben, and not on Tripod.</p>
<p>So if you watch TV like me, you should try <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/skipper-tv-movie-tracker/id6780667490">Skipper</a>. And if you&#8217;re not like me, you should try <a href="https://apps.apple.com/redeem?ctx=offercodes&#038;id=6824844745&#038;code=WELCOME&#038;ref=benricemccarthy.ghost.io">Tripod</a>. That&#8217;s the beauty of these app niches; they don&#8217;t have to be all things to all people, they just need to find their audience and serve them well.</p>
